# Live + dev databases — NEVER commit these
*.db
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
server/fleshmarket.db

# Env / secrets
.env

# Deps / noise
node_modules/
npm-debug.log*
*.log
# ANY package-lock, not just the server's. A stale one shipped inside a build zip
# once cost a multi-hour outage and a DB restore from the cron backup, and the
# rule was written as server/package-lock.json, which stopped covering the case
# the moment a second package.json existed at the repo root.
package-lock.json

# SQLite sidecar files (WAL mode) - never commit these either
*.db-shm
*.db-wal
*.db-journal

# ── Licence-restricted art: deployed, never committed ────────────────────────
# THREE OF FOUR ART PACKS FORBID REDISTRIBUTING THE ART, and this repo is
# public. That is not bad luck with three packs; "use it in your game, do not
# redistribute the source art" is what nearly every commercial art licence
# says, so fixing it pack by pack would have kept failing.
#
# So the art stops living in git. ship.sh pushes these directories to the VPS
# over ssh as a separate step, after the git push, so the live game has the art
# and the public repo never has. A clone without them still runs: reach-battle
# falls back to wireframe ground and wireframe units when a sheet is absent,
# which was built as a load-failure path and turns out to be the thing that
# makes this workable.
#
# WHAT IS NOT HERE, DELIBERATELY: client/assets/space/nature/ is CC0 and stays
# tracked, and so does everything under client/assets/space/troops/. If a pack's
# licence is settled, its art belongs in the repo like any other source.
#
# apply.sh removes TRACKED files only, so a local build keeps these across an
# apply and the zip refreshes them. Nothing about the local workflow changes.
client/assets/space/terrain/*.png
client/assets/space/vehicles/
client/assets/space/brood/

# citybattle bench: harness frames are build output, not source
tools/_citybattle/
